---
import dataset from '../data/dataset.json'
import { median } from '../lib/metrics.js'

// Beat 3 prints no field of any row. Every figure here is derived from all of them, so it
// is taken from dataset.json, which keeps the unrounded fit, and the copy test checks the
// printed digit against results2.json as well. The two agree at one decimal place.
const fitted = dataset.events.filter((e) => e.powR2 !== null && e.expR2 !== null)
const powWins = fitted.filter((e) => e.powR2 > e.expR2).length

// The median of an even-length set is the mean of the two middle values. Reaching for the
// upper of the two instead moves the exponent from 1.283 to 1.285 and the day-thirty
// figure from 21.5 to 21.4, which is a rounding rule choosing a published digit.
const alpha = median(fitted.map((e) => e.powAlpha))

// Under V = C t^-alpha, a curve at day t is at half that value at t * 2^(1/alpha). That is
// where the clock lands, not how long it took, and the elapsed time is that less t. Both
// figures below are elapsed, so the two are the same quantity measured at two starts.
const halveFrom = (t) => (t * (2 ** (1 / alpha) - 1)).toFixed(1)
---

<section class="beat">
  <p class="body">
    This page is called Attention Half-Life. The data does not show a half-life.
  </p>

  <p class="body">
    A power law fits the fall better than an exponential on {powWins} of {fitted.length} events.
    The fit runs from the day after the peak to day thirty. Under a power law the halving
    time is not constant. It grows.
  </p>

  <p class="body">
    At the median exponent the same curve takes {halveFrom(1)} days to halve when measured
    from day one, and {halveFrom(30)} days when measured from day thirty.
  </p>

  <p class="body">
    A half-life is one number that holds wherever you start counting. This fall has no such
    number.
  </p>
</section>

<style>
  .beat {
    padding-bottom: 6rem;
  }

  .body {
    max-width: var(--measure);
    margin: 2rem 0 0;
    font-size: 1.06rem;
  }
</style>
